fact WorkItem(id: "WI-978", description: some(value: "FOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-975 (see docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md). The\nload-time BACKING OBLIGATION for a provision does not reach a provision declared\ninside a NAMESPACE AT A SORT'S ADDRESS (059 calls that a \"secondary entry\" to the\nsort's scope; `entity Rec(...)` beside `namespace Rec ... end`).\n\nMEASURED, with the detector \"declare the provision with NO backing and see\nwhether the load is refused\":\n\n  namespace y                              -- fires\n    sort Show { sort T = ?; operation show(x: T) -> Int64 }\n    entity Rec(n: Int64)\n    fact Show[T = Rec]\n  end\n  => REFUSED: 'y.Rec' provides 'y.Show' but backs no operation 'y.Show.show'\n     (no default on 'y.Show', no own 'show' on 'y.Rec')\n\n  ...the SAME fact moved inside `namespace Rec ... end`  => LOADS CLEAN.\n  ...and it still loads clean with the backing member sitting right beside it.\n\nSo the obligation is skipped for that placement, both when it would have fired\nand when it would have passed. The check is not reporting a different answer --\nit never runs.\n\nLIKELY MECHANISM (not confirmed): the provision scan walks declared sort BODIES,\ni.e. `Item::SortWithBody` items; a secondary entry's items arrive under an\n`Item::Namespace`, so they are never visited.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and the FIRST thing to pin: whether a provision declared there\nparticipates in INSTANCE DISPATCH at all, or only misses the check. The\ngeneric-caller fixture built to test it (`operation useIt[T](x: T) -> Int64\nrequires Show[T] = Show.show(x)`) failed identically for BOTH placements\n(\"operation has no body: z.Show.show\"), so it measured nothing. Answer that\nbefore choosing the fix: if dispatch also misses the provision, the defect is\nlarger than a missing check.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: proposal 059 R3 explicitly ALLOWS `fact Spec[X]` and host\n`provides ... language ... end` blocks in a secondary entry -- that placement is\nmost of the point of the mechanism (a secondary entry supplying members for a foreign\ncarrier must be able to carry the provision for them). It is allowed into a\nplacement the obligation scan skips, so a carrier can claim a spec with nothing\nbacking it and load clean.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the obligation fires for a provision declared in a secondary entry,\nwith the same message it gives one level out; a test drives BOTH placements with\nbacking absent (both refused) and present (both accepted) -- the both-sides pair\nis the control, since a test that only asserts the refusal passes on an\nimplementation that refuses every provision. State at the test site which half\nfails when the fix is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T03:58:27Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-978", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The ticket's LIKELY MECHANISM was refuted; the real one is narrower\nand order-dependent.\n\nNOT the obligation scan. It does not walk `Item::SortWithBody` items — it walks\nthe `SortProvidesInfo` FACTS in the loaded KB (`check_provider_operations`,\ntyping.rs). It was never skipped for a secondary entry: measured, it already\nfired for a `sort X` main entry AND for an `enum X` one, and for a free-standing\n`entity X` when the `namespace X` block was written FIRST.\n\nThe EMITTER dropped it, keyed on DECLARATION ORDER. `maybe_emit_fact_provides_info`\n(load.rs) branched on `kind_of(domain)` = `primary_kind()` = the FIRST category\nadded to the symbol, while a secondary entry's symbol carries a SET of them\n(WI-956/WI-926). Measured, beside a `namespace X` block:\n\n    sort X … end     primary = Sort       has_kind(Sort) = true\n    enum X … end     primary = Sort       has_kind(Sort) = true\n    entity X(…)      primary = ENTITY     has_kind(Sort) = true\n\nSo a claim in a secondary entry to a §6.3 free-standing entity matched NEITHER\narm and fell out of the `_ => return`: no `SortProvidesInfo` was emitted at all.\nThat answers the ticket's \"FIRST thing to pin\" — the provision did not merely\nmiss the check, IT DID NOT EXIST, so every reader missed it, dispatch included.\nThe defect was larger than a missing check, but in the other direction: one\nemitter, not a scan that needed teaching about a new item shape.\n\nFIX: `has_kind(domain, Sort)` — \"does this scope name a type\" — the reading 059\nitself prescribes. One branch, one site (the only `kind_of(domain)` in load.rs).\n\nA SECOND POPULATION, found by making the fallthrough LOUD rather than by\nreasoning about it: a `fact Spec[X]` at a FILE'S TOP LEVEL, whose domain is the\nsynthetic `_global` root scope — no declared category at all, so `Namespace` was\nas false for it as `Sort` was. It emitted no provision either, while the\nidentical text one `namespace` in did. Hence the else is \"everything else\", not\n`has_kind(…, Namespace)`: the classification is binary, and there is no third\ncase left to fall out of. `parse_test::load_polynom_with_ring_requirement` is\nthat fixture; it fails loudly against the three-arm spelling.\n\nTESTS: `rustland/anthill-core/tests/wi978_secondary_entry_provision_test.rs`,\n5 tests. The 2x2x2 acceptance matrix (entity/sort carrier x claim in-entry/one-\nlevel-out x backing absent/present) is split into two tests that report EVERY\nbad row rather than panicking on the first, so backing the fix out names the\ndiscriminating row exactly. MEASURED by reverting the branch: in each half\nexactly ONE of the four rows fails — `entity` carrier, claim IN THE SECONDARY\nENTRY. The unbacked half loads clean; the BACKED half also loads clean and\nrecords `[]`, which is why the test counts provisions instead of reading the\nverdict. The other three cells pass either way, by design, and are the control.\n`plain_namespace_provision_still_derives_its_carrier` pins the arm the fix must\nnot have widened; it too passes either way.\n\nFull workspace: 4118 passed, 0 failed (rustland/scripts/test.sh).\n\nDOCS: the rule is in `docs/kernel-language.md` §6.3, on the WI-931 bullet it\ncompletes (\"the obligation follows the claim, not where it is written or which\ndeclaration came first\"). 059's table row and R3 paragraph corrected — they said\nthe check does not run; it runs, the emitter did not feed it.\n\nSPUN OFF as WI-1008, measured here and deliberately not folded in: with the\nprovision now recorded, a generic caller reaching the carrier through\n`requires Show[T = T]` STILL dies `OperationBodyMissing` when the backing\noperation is declared in the secondary entry — and identically whether the claim\nsits in the entry or one level out, while the same caller answers 111 with the\noperation in the SORT BODY. The OP's placement is that discriminator, not the\nclaim's. It is dispatch-side, in another subsystem, and needs a decision\n(widen dispatch vs. narrow `op_backed`, the latter contradicting 059 R2).", at: "2026-08-05T03:58:29Z")
